R36-I DRIFT SWEEP (POSITIVE) — nswrite write-budget — s2/lifecycle_bakeoff.py
Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B, RTX 4090, 12 rounds x 40, 1 seed, PYTHONHASHSEED=0. nswrite (input-only null-space).
Hypothesis: nswrite's residual forgetting is per-step DRIFT accumulation, not wrong-target. Test: write
LESS per stream (fewer steps / lower LR) and check retention AT MATCHED newest.

================= 12-STREAM RESULT (all-seen / newest) =================
  config (LD_STEPS / BK_NS_LR)        all-seen   all-para   newest   eff-grad(final)
  1000 / 1.5e-4  (baseline)           0.474      0.352      0.85     0.237
   300 / 1.5e-4                       0.590      0.462      0.97     0.291
   100 / 1.5e-4                       0.602      0.479      0.97     0.341
   300 / 7.5e-5  (best)               0.652      0.573      0.97     0.339

VERDICT — DRIFT IS THE LEVER (clean positive, confound rejected).
- Writing less gives +0.18 all-seen (0.474 -> 0.652) AND HIGHER newest (0.85 -> 0.97). Newest went UP,
  not down, so this is NOT under-fitting (codex's confound) — it is genuine drift reduction.
- Mechanism: LD_STEPS=1000 massively over-writes a 40-fact stream (learnable in ~100-300 steps); the
  excess ~700 steps just drift old (and even recent) knowledge. Fewer steps + lower LR write the fact
  and stop before drifting. Lower LR (7.5e-5) helps beyond fewer steps alone (0.652 vs 0.590 at 300).
- fresh_diagonal ~0.95-0.97 across streams for the reduced-budget configs (newest never sacrificed).

IMPLICATION: rehearsal-free retention improves substantially (0.474 -> 0.652 at 12 streams, vs replay
~0.91) purely by write-BUDGETING, no new mechanism, no replay, no growth. This is the drift branch:
next = adaptive write-budget (early-stop each stream when its recall hits a target) + verify at 24
streams. If it holds at 24, the project's rehearsal-free claim strengthens: interference-aware writing +
minimal write budget closes much of the gap to replay with a single dense checkpoint and no memory.
